## Switching Between Route Table Modes

{% if manage_route_table %}
### Switching from Manual → Terraform-managed

If you previously applied in Manual mode and now want Terraform to own the
route tables, import them into state first:

```bash
terraform import \
  module.primary.oci_core_default_route_table.cluster_rt \
  {{ primary_route_table_id or '<primary-default-rt-ocid>' }}

terraform import \
  module.dr.oci_core_default_route_table.cluster_rt \
  {{ standby_route_table_id or '<dr-default-rt-ocid>' }}
```

Then follow the *Read → Update → Verify* steps in *Route Table Management*
above before running `terraform apply`.

{% else %}
### Switching from Terraform-managed → Manual (your current mode)

If you previously applied in Terraform-managed mode (or ran `terraform import`
on the route tables), the resources are still tracked in Terraform state even
though the `resource` block no longer exists in `main.tf`.

**Terraform will plan to destroy or modify the route tables** on the next apply
unless you remove them from state first.

Run this **before** your next `terraform apply`:

```bash
terraform state rm 'module.primary.oci_core_default_route_table.cluster_rt'
terraform state rm 'module.dr.oci_core_default_route_table.cluster_rt'
```

After removing from state, Terraform treats the route tables as unmanaged.
`terraform plan` will show no changes to them and `terraform apply` will skip
them entirely.

> **Verify with `terraform plan`** after running `state rm` — the output should
> contain no reference to `oci_core_default_route_table`. If you still see it,
> check for additional state entries with:
> `terraform state list | grep route_table`

{% endif %}

